1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,820
That was the fourth of the CTO's questions
at the start of this module, and the

2
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:09,759
answer was "yes".

3
00:00:10,099 --> 00:00:15,088
Here's how it happens. A rebase copies
your commits, your saved snapshots, onto a

4
00:00:15,128 --> 00:00:19,993
new starting point, and this one stops
at a conflict: Git can't decide a file's

5
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:23,532
content. The developer wants
his own version of the file.

6
00:00:23,572 --> 00:00:27,506
He types the option that says
"ours", because the change is his.

7
00:00:27,546 --> 00:00:31,790
He stages the file, continues, and
Git prints "Successfully rebased".

8
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:35,330
No error, no warning. His
commit is gone from the branch.

9
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:41,014
He did nothing careless. He trusted a word.
Today you'll learn what that word means

10
00:00:41,054 --> 00:00:45,555
during a rebase, and why Git stayed
silent. Keep that silence in mind.

11
00:00:45,595 --> 00:00:46,463
It comes back.

12
00:00:48,303 --> 00:00:52,075
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:52,115 --> 00:00:57,198
A rebase replays your commits one at a
time, and each replay is a three-way merge:

14
00:00:57,238 --> 00:01:02,010
two versions combined against their
common base. So each replay can conflict.

15
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:06,884
The merge module showed how to read a
conflict: markers in the file, and three

16
00:01:06,924 --> 00:01:11,070
stages in the index, the file that
holds your proposed next commit.

17
00:01:11,410 --> 00:01:16,126
What changes in a rebase is who is who.
The two sides are swapped compared with a

18
00:01:16,166 --> 00:01:20,471
merge, and almost half of the developers
asked in one poll didn't know it.

19
00:01:20,511 --> 00:01:24,228
So you're in good company. That
swap is the first half of today.

20
00:01:24,568 --> 00:01:29,122
The second half is the mirror image.
Sometimes a commit disappears from a rebased

21
00:01:29,162 --> 00:01:32,978
branch on purpose, because the new
base already contains its change.

22
00:01:33,018 --> 00:01:36,664
Git notices that in two different
ways, and you can predict both.

23
00:01:36,704 --> 00:01:41,371
When you can tell the intended disappearance
from the accidental one, you can trust

24
00:01:41,411 --> 00:01:42,222
a rebase again.

25
00:01:44,062 --> 00:01:45,640
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:47,393
After this video you can:

27
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:52,768
Name the base, ours and theirs of each step
of a rebase, and explain why the sides

28
00:01:52,808 --> 00:01:53,697
appear swapped.

29
00:01:54,037 --> 00:01:56,959
Inspect the commit being applied
and resolve its conflict.

30
00:01:57,299 --> 00:02:01,087
Describe two ways a rebase conflict
loses work without any error.

31
00:02:01,427 --> 00:02:06,605
Choose between --continue,
--skip, --abort and --quit.

32
00:02:06,945 --> 00:02:11,257
Predict which commits a rebase drops because
the upstream already has the change.

33
00:02:13,097 --> 00:02:17,444
In one sentence: during a rebase, "ours"
is the new base plus the copies made so

34
00:02:17,484 --> 00:02:21,941
far, and "theirs" is your own commit, the
one being replayed: the opposite of what

35
00:02:21,981 --> 00:02:22,929
the words suggest.

36
00:02:23,269 --> 00:02:28,613
Precisely. Each replay is a three-way merge
into HEAD, Git's name for where you are.

37
00:02:28,653 --> 00:02:33,357
Stage 2, "ours", is HEAD: the upstream
commit plus what has been replayed.

38
00:02:33,397 --> 00:02:38,293
Stage 3, "theirs", is REBASE_HEAD, your
commit. Stage 1 is the parent of your

39
00:02:38,333 --> 00:02:42,780
commit. The local manual says it in
one line: "the sides are swapped".

40
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:48,468
Why? Recall the four steps of a rebase.
Step 2 detached HEAD at the upstream, the

41
00:02:48,508 --> 00:02:53,856
tip of main. From then on, HEAD is the
upstream's history growing by your copies.

42
00:02:53,896 --> 00:02:59,044
On screen, one copy is made, and the
rebase has stopped at your second commit.

43
00:02:59,084 --> 00:03:02,237
"Ours" always means HEAD.
It never meant "mine".

44
00:03:02,577 --> 00:03:03,788
The ways out of a stop:

45
00:03:04,128 --> 00:03:08,610
The textbook labels the first four
🟡 CAUTION and the last 🟢 SAFE.

46
00:03:08,650 --> 00:03:14,037
Remember from video 53 that --abort also
discards resolution work in progress.

47
00:03:14,377 --> 00:03:19,985
Now section 9.12. In one sentence: a rebase
leaves out commits whose change the new

48
00:03:20,025 --> 00:03:25,497
base already contains, and it notices in
two different ways: before replaying, by

49
00:03:25,537 --> 00:03:30,669
patch ID, and after replaying, by an
empty result. A patch ID is a hash of a

50
00:03:30,709 --> 00:03:35,160
change's diff: same change, same
patch ID, whatever the commit ID.

51
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:40,125
The first mechanism compares patch
IDs before the list is written.

52
00:03:40,165 --> 00:03:44,367
A commit with an equivalent
upstream never gets a pick line.

53
00:03:44,407 --> 00:03:48,679
On screen that's 3667d58, the
same change as b0f4913 on main.

54
00:03:48,719 --> 00:03:54,121
The second mechanism is governed by
--empty. A replay that changes nothing is

55
00:03:54,161 --> 00:03:59,847
dropped by default. keep records an empty
commit. stop pauses so that you decide.

56
00:03:59,887 --> 00:04:01,826
With -i the default is stop.

57
00:04:02,166 --> 00:04:05,140
And here's why Git was
silent in the opening story.

58
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:09,985
A replay with an empty result normally
means the change is already upstream, which

59
00:04:10,025 --> 00:04:14,535
is the common and harmless case. Git
can't tell that case from "the developer

60
00:04:14,575 --> 00:04:17,430
resolved the conflict by
throwing his side away".

61
00:04:19,270 --> 00:04:23,977
The textbook's analogy: a rebase checks out
the other side's latest commit and then

62
00:04:24,017 --> 00:04:28,380
merges your commits into it, one at a
time. In each of those merges the house

63
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:31,697
belongs to the other side, and
your commit is the visitor.

64
00:04:32,037 --> 00:04:37,097
The analogy is almost literal. It breaks
only if you think of "ours" as a person.

65
00:04:37,137 --> 00:04:39,238
It's a position: whatever HEAD is.

66
00:04:39,578 --> 00:04:43,210
A practical rule follows, and it
doesn't need the words at all.

67
00:04:43,250 --> 00:04:47,873
Read the label after the closing marker.
In a rebase conflict, the lower half is

68
00:04:47,913 --> 00:04:51,778
labelled with the ID and subject
of a commit. That commit is yours.

69
00:04:51,818 --> 00:04:55,624
The upper half says HEAD. That's
where you're being replayed onto.

70
00:04:57,464 --> 00:05:02,876
The same conflict, drawn twice. On the
left, as a merge: ours is your branch, with

71
00:05:02,916 --> 00:05:08,196
20, and theirs is main, with 8. On the
right, as step two of the rebase: ours is

72
00:05:08,236 --> 00:05:13,315
HEAD, with 8, and theirs is your commit,
with 20. The base says 5 both times.

73
00:05:15,155 --> 00:05:16,411
Part 1: who is who.

74
00:05:16,751 --> 00:05:22,804
Upper half, labelled HEAD: 8. Lower half,
labelled 569e6c9 (Fetch 20 candidates for

75
00:05:22,844 --> 00:05:28,823
the reranker): 20. Quick quiz, two options.
Which half is your work: the upper, or

76
00:05:28,863 --> 00:05:30,805
the lower? Say it out loud.

77
00:05:35,645 --> 00:05:43,136
The lower half is yours. Now the index:
stage 1 says 5, stage 2 says 8, stage 3 says

78
00:05:43,176 --> 00:05:43,405
20.

79
00:05:43,745 --> 00:05:49,667
Stage 2, "ours", is HEAD: the upstream commit
439e4c6 plus the one copy made so far.

80
00:05:49,707 --> 00:05:55,557
Stage 3, "theirs", is REBASE_HEAD: your
commit. Now integrate the same two branches

81
00:05:55,597 --> 00:06:01,021
with a merge, from the same branch.
Predict which value is in the upper half.

82
00:06:01,061 --> 00:06:01,731
I'll wait.

83
00:06:06,571 --> 00:06:08,134
20 on top, 8 below.

84
00:06:08,474 --> 00:06:13,402
Same two values, opposite positions. The
demo aborts the merge and goes back to the

85
00:06:13,442 --> 00:06:14,300
stopped rebase.

86
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:21,278
Part 2: inspect and resolve. labs/run
ch09/conflict-inspect shows what you can ask

87
00:06:21,318 --> 00:06:22,744
before you decide.

88
00:06:23,084 --> 00:06:28,217
git rebase --show-current-patch prints the
commit being replayed: what it intended,

89
00:06:28,257 --> 00:06:32,268
against its own parent. From 5 to
20, and a call to the reranker.

90
00:06:32,308 --> 00:06:34,698
That's the intent you have to preserve.

91
00:06:35,038 --> 00:06:39,692
To resolve, put the content you want
into the file, stage it, and continue.

92
00:06:39,732 --> 00:06:44,950
Taking your own version whole is --theirs
here. git restore --theirs 🔴 DANGEROUS, as

93
00:06:44,990 --> 00:06:50,145
in the merge module. It overwrites the
working tree file with the whole of stage 3.

94
00:06:50,185 --> 00:06:54,651
Your hand edits to that file are lost.
The preview is git diff --theirs.

95
00:06:54,691 --> 00:06:59,157
And it's appropriate when the whole
file from that side is the decision.

96
00:06:59,497 --> 00:07:04,019
Look at the status before continuing:
M, staged. There's something to commit.

97
00:07:04,059 --> 00:07:05,204
Remember this check.

98
00:07:05,544 --> 00:07:10,094
--continue commits the index as the
copy of the stopped commit and runs the

99
00:07:10,134 --> 00:07:15,173
remaining instructions. The reflog, Git's
local record of where HEAD has been, says

100
00:07:15,213 --> 00:07:19,885
rebase (continue) for that step. Three
commits on the branch, on top of main.

101
00:07:20,225 --> 00:07:25,116
If you know in advance which side should
win every conflicting hunk, each block of

102
00:07:25,156 --> 00:07:28,183
changed lines, say so, and
the rebase doesn't stop.

103
00:07:28,523 --> 00:07:33,620
-X theirs favours your commits. -X ours
would favour main and discard your side of

104
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:36,188
every conflicting hunk without a message.

105
00:07:36,528 --> 00:07:41,704
Part 3: the other exits.
labs/run ch09/conflict-exits.

106
00:07:42,044 --> 00:07:48,205
--skip gives a branch without the commit,
and with the value from main: two commits,

107
00:07:48,245 --> 00:07:50,789
TOP_K = 8, no call to the reranker.

108
00:07:51,129 --> 00:07:56,006
--quit removes the state directory, the
rebase's own folder inside dot git, and

109
00:07:56,046 --> 00:08:01,109
nothing else. The branch is untouched, HEAD
is detached on the half-built history,

110
00:08:01,149 --> 00:08:03,536
and the index still holds the conflict.

111
00:08:03,876 --> 00:08:08,407
Getting back to the branch from there
takes a git reset --hard 🔴 to clear the

112
00:08:08,447 --> 00:08:13,335
conflict, on a state you have decided to
discard, and Git warns that you're leaving

113
00:08:13,375 --> 00:08:18,203
a commit behind. Use --quit only when you
want to keep that state and take over by

114
00:08:18,243 --> 00:08:18,500
hand.

115
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:21,058
Two refusals, from conflict-inspect:

116
00:08:25,598 --> 00:08:30,592
The first: nothing is staged yet. The
second: a rebase is already running.

117
00:08:30,632 --> 00:08:36,171
Take the first suggestion of that message,
never the rm -fr, unless git status has

118
00:08:36,211 --> 00:08:38,416
told you there's nothing to keep.

119
00:08:38,756 --> 00:08:45,150
Part 4: two traps. labs/run ch09/conflict-traps.
The first is the opening story.

120
00:08:45,190 --> 00:08:50,780
Stopped at the same commit, you want
your version, so you type --ours.

121
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:52,549
Watch the status line.

122
00:08:52,889 --> 00:08:57,607
git status --short printed nothing.
Then "Successfully rebased".

123
00:08:57,647 --> 00:08:59,615
Predict the log. I'll wait.

124
00:09:04,455 --> 00:09:08,440
Two commits. "Fetch 20 candidates
for the reranker" is not there.

125
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:13,898
git range-diff, which the next video explains,
shows it in one line: commit 2 of the

126
00:09:13,938 --> 00:09:18,811
old branch has no counterpart. Almost
everyone falls into this once, because the

127
00:09:18,851 --> 00:09:19,793
word invites it.

128
00:09:20,133 --> 00:09:24,147
That's the silence from the opening:
an empty step, dropped by default.

129
00:09:24,487 --> 00:09:29,395
The second trap is a habit from ordinary
work. This time the resolution is right,

130
00:09:29,435 --> 00:09:31,105
but the next command is not.

131
00:09:37,245 --> 00:09:42,754
At a conflict stop, HEAD is the previous
copy. git commit --amend therefore folded

132
00:09:42,794 --> 00:09:48,235
the resolution into "Add reranker skeleton",
which now changes two files, and the

133
00:09:48,275 --> 00:09:53,919
stopped commit, left with nothing, was
dropped. After a conflict the sequence is git

134
00:09:53,959 --> 00:09:59,468
add, then git rebase --continue. --amend
belongs to an edit stop, where Git itself

135
00:09:59,508 --> 00:10:00,280
suggests it.

136
00:10:00,620 --> 00:10:05,107
Part 5: commits that are already
upstream. labs/run ch09/upstream-picked.

137
00:10:05,147 --> 00:10:10,193
main received the middle commit of your
branch through a cherry-pick, which copies

138
00:10:10,233 --> 00:10:13,666
one commit's change onto
another branch as a new commit.

139
00:10:14,006 --> 00:10:20,405
"Retry flaky downloads three times" exists
twice: 3667d58 on your branch, b0f4913 on

140
00:10:20,445 --> 00:10:26,767
main. Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds:
draw feat/ingest after git rebase main.

141
00:10:26,807 --> 00:10:29,526
How many commits of yours are on it?

142
00:10:31,866 --> 00:10:36,994
git cherry marks with a minus each of your
commits that has an equivalent upstream

143
00:10:37,034 --> 00:10:42,036
and with a plus each that has none. git
log --cherry-mark puts an equals sign on

144
00:10:42,076 --> 00:10:46,952
both members of the pair. Both compare
patch IDs, and the rebase runs the same

145
00:10:46,992 --> 00:10:49,283
comparison before it writes the list.

146
00:10:49,623 --> 00:10:54,733
"Skipped previously applied commit 3667d58",
and only two commits are replayed.

147
00:10:55,073 --> 00:10:59,275
Check your drawing: two, not three. This
disappearance is announced, and it was

148
00:10:59,315 --> 00:10:59,920
predictable.

149
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:06,109
Second case. labs/run ch09/upstream-squashed.
Your first two commits reached main as

150
00:11:06,149 --> 00:11:10,246
one squashed commit, two commits
combined into one new one.

151
00:11:10,586 --> 00:11:15,366
git cherry marks all three with a plus:
no single commit upstream has the diff of

152
00:11:15,406 --> 00:11:18,579
either of yours. So the first
mechanism finds nothing.

153
00:11:18,619 --> 00:11:22,090
Predict what the rebase does
with the first two. I'll wait.

154
00:11:26,930 --> 00:11:31,565
Both were replayed, both changed nothing,
because their content is already there,

155
00:11:31,605 --> 00:11:35,374
and both were dropped as empty:
"patch contents already upstream".

156
00:11:35,414 --> 00:11:36,990
That's the second mechanism.

157
00:11:37,330 --> 00:11:43,050
With -i the default is --empty=stop: the
rebase pauses at each commit that became

158
00:11:43,090 --> 00:11:44,899
empty and lets you decide.

159
00:11:45,239 --> 00:11:48,552
--reapply-cherry-picks switches
the first mechanism off.

160
00:11:48,592 --> 00:11:51,724
The commit is then replayed
and caught by the second.

161
00:11:57,264 --> 00:12:02,329
Where it stops working: "already upstream"
means the same diff, or a replay with an

162
00:12:02,369 --> 00:12:07,249
empty result. If the upstream version
differs from yours, for instance because a

163
00:12:07,289 --> 00:12:12,169
reviewer's change went into the squash,
neither mechanism applies and the replay

164
00:12:12,209 --> 00:12:17,335
conflicts. That was the stacked branch of
video 54, and the answer there was to name

165
00:12:17,375 --> 00:12:18,872
the boundary with --onto.

166
00:12:20,712 --> 00:12:22,360
Five mistakes to watch for.

167
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:27,893
Taking --ours in a rebase to keep your own
change. Root cause: "ours" is HEAD, the

168
00:12:27,933 --> 00:12:33,189
side you are rebasing onto; the index then
equals HEAD, the replay is empty, and an

169
00:12:33,229 --> 00:12:35,869
empty commit is dropped without a message.

170
00:12:36,209 --> 00:12:40,067
Running git commit --amend after
resolving a rebase conflict.

171
00:12:40,107 --> 00:12:45,179
Root cause: at a conflict stop HEAD is
the previous copy, so the amend folds the

172
00:12:45,219 --> 00:12:49,269
resolution into that commit and
leaves the stopped commit empty.

173
00:12:49,609 --> 00:12:54,511
Running --continue without reading git
status. Root cause: a clean status after git

174
00:12:54,551 --> 00:12:59,393
add means there is nothing to commit for
this step, which is the sign of the first

175
00:12:59,433 --> 00:12:59,869
mistake.

176
00:13:00,209 --> 00:13:06,957
Following the rm -fr .git/rebase-merge
suggestion. Root cause: it removes the state

177
00:13:06,997 --> 00:13:13,582
of a rebase that may still hold something
valuable; --continue, --abort or --skip

178
00:13:13,622 --> 00:13:14,727
are the exits.

179
00:13:15,067 --> 00:13:18,706
Assuming git cherry predicts
every drop after a squash merge.

180
00:13:18,746 --> 00:13:23,652
Root cause: the patch-ID comparison needs
an upstream commit with the same diff; a

181
00:13:23,692 --> 00:13:28,296
squash is caught only later, as an empty
replay, and not at all if the squash

182
00:13:28,336 --> 00:13:29,864
differs from your commits.

183
00:13:31,704 --> 00:13:37,641
Now, out of the lab. The textbook cites
two figures from Julia Evans's 2024 polls,

184
00:13:37,681 --> 00:13:43,618
with their caveat. 48 percent of 1,511
respondents did not know that the two sides

185
00:13:43,658 --> 00:13:49,595
swap between merge and rebase. And 61
percent of about 1,480 had seen a production

186
00:13:49,635 --> 00:13:54,843
bug caused by a bad conflict resolution.
Both samples are self-selected.

187
00:13:54,883 --> 00:14:00,382
Its conclusion is the useful part: assume
the confusion exists on your team.

188
00:14:00,722 --> 00:14:06,004
A second cost is specific to rebase. A
branch of twelve commits that all touch the

189
00:14:06,044 --> 00:14:11,325
lines main changed can stop twelve times.
Each stop is a chance for one of today's

190
00:14:11,365 --> 00:14:15,219
traps. The textbook gives three
ways to reduce the exposure.

191
00:14:15,259 --> 00:14:19,502
Reduce the number of replays first,
by squashing with --keep-base.

192
00:14:19,542 --> 00:14:23,396
Or record resolutions with
rerere, which Chapter 14C covers.

193
00:14:23,436 --> 00:14:25,213
Or integrate with one merge.

194
00:14:25,553 --> 00:14:30,500
And for follow-up branches after a squash
merge: a plain git rebase main does the

195
00:14:30,540 --> 00:14:34,624
right thing exactly when the squash
equals the sum of your commits.

196
00:14:34,664 --> 00:14:39,672
Check with git cherry -v main first. For
lines marked with a plus that you believe

197
00:14:39,712 --> 00:14:42,689
are merged, expect an empty
replay or a conflict.

198
00:14:44,529 --> 00:14:50,570
Your turn. Do Lab 9.4, "A conflict
in a rebase, and who is "ours"", in

199
00:14:50,610 --> 00:14:52,742
lab-manual/m09-rebase.md.

200
00:14:53,082 --> 00:14:56,614
When the rebase stops, before
you touch the file, write down:

201
00:14:56,954 --> 00:15:02,625
The content of stage 1, stage 2 and stage
3, and for each, which commit it comes

202
00:15:02,665 --> 00:15:02,982
from.

203
00:15:03,322 --> 00:15:07,200
Which of --ours and --theirs
would give you your own version.

204
00:15:07,540 --> 00:15:12,290
What git status --short will print after a
correct resolution and git add, and what

205
00:15:12,330 --> 00:15:14,310
it would print after the wrong one.

206
00:15:14,650 --> 00:15:18,069
Then resolve, and check your third
prediction before you continue.

207
00:15:18,409 --> 00:15:25,021
The challenge is Exercise 9.8, Level 3,
"the same conflict at every commit", in

208
00:15:25,061 --> 00:15:27,800
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

209
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:35,172
Q148: "During a rebase, what do --ours
and --theirs refer to, and why?

210
00:15:35,212 --> 00:15:40,345
Give one concrete way this loses
work without any error message."

211
00:15:42,685 --> 00:15:47,450
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
derives the meaning of the two words from

212
00:15:47,490 --> 00:15:52,375
where HEAD is during a rebase, so that the
swap is a consequence and not a rule to

213
00:15:52,415 --> 00:15:57,240
memorize. For the second half it tells the
sequence command by command, and names

214
00:15:57,280 --> 00:16:02,165
the exact point at which the work is lost
and the mechanism that makes Git silent.

215
00:16:02,205 --> 00:16:07,210
It ends with the two checks that would have
caught it, one before continuing and one

216
00:16:07,250 --> 00:16:08,231
after the rebase.

217
00:16:10,071 --> 00:16:14,198
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

218
00:16:14,538 --> 00:16:18,101
Each step of a rebase is a
three-way merge into HEAD.

219
00:16:18,141 --> 00:16:23,743
The base is the parent of my commit, "ours"
is the upstream plus the copies so far,

220
00:16:23,783 --> 00:16:29,113
and "theirs" is my commit being replayed.
So --theirs is my version, and --ours

221
00:16:29,153 --> 00:16:34,619
followed by --continue drops my commit
silently, because the replay became empty.

222
00:16:34,959 --> 00:16:41,057
After a conflict the sequence is git add
and git rebase --continue, never --amend.

223
00:16:41,097 --> 00:16:46,970
--skip leaves the commit out, --abort
returns to the start, and --quit leaves a

224
00:16:47,010 --> 00:16:48,991
detached, half-built state.

225
00:16:49,331 --> 00:16:54,191
A rebase also drops commits on purpose:
by patch ID before replaying, which git

226
00:16:54,231 --> 00:16:57,540
cherry predicts, and as empty
results after replaying.

227
00:16:59,380 --> 00:17:03,492
Read sections 9.11 and 9.12 of Chapter 9.

228
00:17:03,832 --> 00:17:09,015
Today you learned to read a rebase conflict
by position, not by the word, and to

229
00:17:09,055 --> 00:17:14,172
tell a planned disappearance from an
accident. Practise Lab 9.4 before the next

230
00:17:14,212 --> 00:17:19,394
video. Next time: git pull --rebase, and
reviewing a rebase with git range-diff.

231
00:17:19,434 --> 00:17:22,789
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

232
00:17:22,829 --> 00:17:24,356
See you in the next one.
